# release-audit/CHANGELOG

## 20260916

0.7.0: Tell a misuse from a finding, and require of each switch the argument it is named for.

1. + bin/release-audit: MISUSE, exit 2 where the fault is in how it was called. Exit 1 had meant both a finding and a mistake, so a gate could not tell seven repositories needing attention from a mistyped path, which is the one thing the exit status is documented as being for. grep and diff both spell this 0, 1, 2 and the first two already agreed.
2. ~ bin/release-audit: switches() rescues OptionParser::ParseError, which had reached the top as a backtrace and exit 1. The argument error is new here and could have been 2 alone, but then a misuse would exit 2 or 1 by which kind it was, and an unknown switch is the likelier mistake of the two.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: --config, --root and --branch declared with optional!() rather than optional(), so each requires the argument it is named for. A bare --root had been legal and set nothing, falling through to the configured root and sweeping everything, which is not what anyone typing it alone meant. It is a misuse now, and reaches exit 2 by the rescue above.
4. ~ README.md: Exit status, and the usage text, both of which promised 1 and 0 only.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.6.0/0.7.0/, and the date.

0.6.0: Audit named repositories rather than those beneath the root.

1. + bin/release-audit: repository_at(), named_paths() and list_all(). `--root` names the directory the repositories are *in*, so `--root .` from inside a repository globs bin, lib and test, finds no `.git` among them and answers "No repositories of ours beneath", which reads as the root being wrong when the root is fine. The only way to audit one was a directory holding a single symlink.
2. + bin/release-audit: an argument is a directory holding a repository, relative or absolute, `~` expanded, `.` being the one you are in. More than one may be named and duplicates collapse. A bare name works where it is a path, which is to say from the root, and reports where it looked when it is not.
3. + bin/release-audit: a named repository skips copy?() and ours?(), which exist to quieten discovery and have nothing to filter when the repository has been named. A fork is reported rather than silently dropped, on the ground that pointing at one is asking for it.
4. + bin/release-audit: naming any repository implies --all, since that is already saying which is wanted, and without it the common case of a clean one prints an empty table.
5. + bin/release-audit: a path which is a directory but not a repository is answered with `--root` naming it, which is what was meant. A path which is not a directory is not, there being nothing there to be a root of.
6. ~ README.md: + the argument under Usage. It is not an option, so the Options table is left alone.
7. ~ README.md: Installation, + 1b symlinked and 1c copied, where 1b had been the copy alone. A symlink makes the command on the path the checkout, which cannot drift from it; a copy is two things kept the same by hand, and outlives the checkout, which a symlink does not. Three ways of installing had been two headings, and the letters are what this README uses for alternatives. Homebrew stays 1a, for anyone wanting a version they did not check out. The checkout blocks carry the $ prompt the rest of the README uses and which 1b had gone without; the one unprefixed line left is what cat answers with, being output rather than a command.
8. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.5.0/0.6.0/, and the date.

## 20260915

0.5.0: Order the listing by standing, and + ROADMAP.md for the questions which are not jobs.

1. + bin/release-audit: standing_rank(), the rows ordered CURRENT, WORKING, OLDER and within each by name. Alphabetical put the two tags disagreeing with origin, which no push corrects, wherever the alphabet did.
2. + ROADMAP.md: whether there is a machine-readable output, whether the checked-out branch is the right scope, where publication belongs, and where the list of repositories comes from. The ordering was the fifth and is done here.
3. ~ README.md: the ordering, and "What it does not report yet" names ROADMAP.md.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.4.0/0.5.0/, and the date.


## 20260913

0.4.0: + the other way a tag and a remote disagree: origin not having it at all.

1. + bin/release-audit: remote_tag_findings() reports a tag which is here and not on origin. It had asked only whether the tags origin holds name the commits ours do, and skipped a tag origin lacked with `next if remote.nil?`, so the one case a push does not correct on its own went unreported. --follow-tags carries only tags reachable from the commits being pushed, so a tag cut onto old history stays here: eventful had seven after a backfill and the check said nothing.
2. - bin/release-audit: REMOTE_TAGS, which asked about the newest five versions only. The cost of the check is the single ls-remote call, which answers with every tag at once, so comparing five or five hundred costs the same; the window was guarding against output volume, which listed() already handles. Dropping it found two divergences it had been hiding, one of them a version already published.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: the usage text, which described one of the two questions --origin now asks.
4. ~ README.md: + the Options table and the two origin findings. "What it does not report yet" named the remote tag check, which --origin has done since 0.3.0.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.3.0/0.4.0/

0.3.0: + --origin, which asks whether the tags origin holds name the commits ours do.

1. + bin/release-audit: remote_tag_findings(), read_remote_refs() and remote_tag_commit(). A push moves commits and leaves tags where they are, so a rewritten history leaves the remote naming commits it has abandoned while every local check reads clean. Behind a switch because it is the only thing here which touches the network.
2. + bin/release-audit: prefetch_remote_refs(), which makes the calls together rather than one after another. Sequentially the run took 5m17s against 12.9s for the whole of the rest of it; sixteen threads bring it to 43.7s, with the findings identical. Each call is spent waiting, and Ruby gives up the lock for the duration of a subprocess.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.2.1/0.3.0/, and the date.

## 20260911

0.2.1: ~ the count: three standings, so that work in progress is not counted as a release left half done.

1. + bin/release-audit: standing(), and WORKING beside CURRENT and OLDER. A release which stopped between being made and being delivered is CURRENT — a latest version with no tag, or commits the remote has not seen. A tree with changes in it is WORKING: it is work rather than a release, though it makes every later column provisional and so is still said. Everything else is OLDER.
2. ~ bin/release-audit: summary() builds its clauses rather than stating one sentence, there being three numbers where there was one and any of them possibly zero.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: a root holding no repositories of ours said `Nothing outstanding in 0 repositories`, which reads as reassurance where nothing was examined. It now says so.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: the constants are in alphabetical order after the defaults.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.2.0/0.2.1/

0.2.0: Separate a repository which has never been tagged from one whose latest release is untagged.

1. + bin/release-audit: untagged_findings(), which distinguishes three states where there was one. A repository with no tags at all says `never tagged, 21 versions claimed` and says it once; a latest claimed version with no tag says so by name, which is the only one of the three that is a release left half done; and versions behind a tagged latest say `4 earlier untagged`, which is backfill and is done years after the fact here by design.
2. - bin/release-audit: untagged_versions(), which nothing calls now.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.2/0.2.0/

0.1.2: ~ the name column: fitted to the longest name reported rather than fixed at 24.

1. + bin/release-audit: name_width() and row(), the width taken from the names actually reported and floored at MINIMUM_NAME_WIDTH so that a short run is not cramped. ROW is gone, a constant being the wrong shape for something computed per run.
2. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.1/0.1.2/

0.1.1: + column headings, the repository column headed only where it is asked for.

1. + bin/release-audit: headings(), and ROW, which the headings and the rows now share so that they cannot drift apart. A run over the estate reports 48 repositories, and reading a column of bare values with nothing saying what they are is what made that overwhelming rather than informative.
2. + bin/release-audit: -n, --name, which heads the repository column. It is off by default: a column of repository names says what it is, and the heading is one more line between the reader and the report.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: - the `<--` before each row's findings, the column now being headed `outstanding`.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: ROW joins the other constants at the top rather than sitting above report().
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.0/0.1.1/

0.1.0: Audit only the repositories which are ours, and say less about the ones which have never been tagged.

1. ~ bin/release-audit: + ours?(), which takes the root commit author as the proxy used where gh is not to hand. A fork cannot be released from here, so every column is meaningless for one: the first run reported homebrew-core with 249 uncommitted files, and rails, sorbet, crystal, direnv and two dotfiles repositories beside it. 171 repositories fall to 86 and 61 findings to 48.
2. ~ bin/release-audit: + copy?(), which now reads .bak, .old and .orig as well as the Finder's " copy". A .bak alongside a repository was reported as a repository of its own.
3. + bin/release-audit: listed(), naming the first six untagged versions and counting the rest. dsv has never been tagged and claims 77 versions, which filled a line and told nobody anything.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: + committed(), so that one file does not read as `1 files`.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.0.0/0.1.0/

0.0.0: Report which repositories beneath a root have a release left half done.

1. + bin/release-audit: a walk for .git directories beneath a root, and per repository three columns which need no network — whether the tree is committed, which versions it claims and has tagged, and whether the branch is pushed. A release passes through five stages; the two which ask the outside world, whether the tag on the remote is the tag here and whether the version is published, are not asked yet.
2. + bin/release-audit: the versions a repository claims are taken from its commit subjects, in the form `1.2.3: summary`. The changelog says it too, in three different filenames and several formats, so it is left for later.
3. + bin/release-audit: the root comes from --root, else the configured one, else the current directory, as tap-audit takes its two directories.
4. + README.md, LICENSE, .gitignore, CHANGELOG.
